{"name":"Peridot","hex":"#e6e200","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"peridot","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A transparent olive green form of olivine, used as a gem.","A yellow-green color, like that of the peridot."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peridot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peridot","blocks":[{"text":"Peridot, sometimes called chrysolite, is a yellow-green transparent variety of olivine, specifically the magnesium rich end member called forsterite. Peridot is one of the few gemstones that occur in only one color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg/330px-Forsterite-Olivine-tmu14a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peridot","wikidata":"Q288732"},{"title":"Peridot (franchise)","blocks":[{"text":"Peridot is a media franchise originally created by Niantic, Inc., and currently owned and operated by Niantic Spatial. The franchise centers on the care and diversification of virtual creatures called Peridots or \"Dots\" for short, utilizing augmented reality (AR) and artificial intelligence (AI) to allow these creatures to interact with users in the real world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/62/Peridot_franchise_logo.png/330px-Peridot_franchise_logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peridot_(franchise)","wikidata":"Q136483794"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png/250px-Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png/250px-Atomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The atomic scale structure of olivine looking along the a axis. Oxygen is shown in red, silicon in pink, and magnesium/iron in blue. A projection of the unit cell is shown by the black rectangle.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAtomic_structure_of_olivine_1.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/500px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/1280px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg/500px-NHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pallasite meteorite with olive-green peridot crystals, found in Fukang, China.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANHM_-_Pallasit_Fukang.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Peridot_Doubling.jpg/500px-Peridot_Doubling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Peridot_Doubling.jpg/1280px-Peridot_Doubling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Peridot_Doubling.jpg/500px-Peridot_Doubling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Due to its high birefringence, doubling of facet junctions is commonly seen when viewing peridot under magnification.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeridot_Doubling.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Peridot_